{
  "id": "Compound::DB04861 - Disease::DOID:10763",
  "verbalization": "Nebivolol is implicated in the pathogenesis of hypertension through a series of interconnected mechanisms involving beta-adrenergic antagonism. The evidence suggests that Nebivolol, through its ability to cause Claudication, which in turn is caused by Acebutolol or Metoprolol, ultimately treats hypertension. This chain of causation highlights a potential role of Nebivolol in the treatment of hypertension via its interaction with beta-adrenergic receptors, a common mechanism in antihypertensive drugs. The shared lowest common ancestors—Beta-Adrenergic Antagonist and Chemical Entity—underscore the structural and functional overlap among these entities, reinforcing the notion of a broader pharmacological relationship. The integration of these pathways suggests that Nebivolol may exert its antihypertensive effects through a mechanism that involves the modulation of adrenergic signaling, potentially offering a novel therapeutic pathway for hypertension treatment.",
